  "state": {
    "case_id": "70",
    "case_summary": "A 38-year-old woman comes to the physician because of a 1-month history of a painless, nonpruritic skin lesion on her right thigh. She initially thought it was an insect bite, but it has slowly increased in size over the past weeks. Her temperature is 36.7 °C (98 °F), pulse is 75/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a 0.8-cm hyperpigmented papule. When the skin lesion is squeezed, the surface retracts inwards. A photograph of the lesion is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. melanoma\nB. basal cell carcinoma\nC. squamous cell carcinoma\nD. dermatofibroma\nE. seborrheic keratosis\nF. None\nG. None\n",
